m.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar700_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"602"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-14T15:49:57-08:00","id":1031858,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1031841#post1031841\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For that to happen women need to be more willing to share parenting, it's win for everyone, Dads get more access to their kids, women get more time for their careers!!!</div> </div> </div> </div>IF a large enough group of men were to march on this subject and protest about it for years on end in a seemingly never-ending battle for the dream of equality, a battle that goes on for decades, then maybe it would be a comparable issue. As it stands, there <i>already is a shift</i> in the way parental rights are handled, at least in the states (and depending on the state). However, stating pay differences for women won't be an issue if men get more access to their children after divorce is ignoring both issues simultaneously and doing both a disservice.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writing up Clanbook: Aabbt","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/700-vysha","name":"Vysha","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar700_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"602"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-14T16:05:56-08:00","id":1031862,"title":"","content":"I wish this Women's March luck, despite my skeptism that Trump will care, I don't want to see him work toward stripping away abortion rights.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-14T16:19:58-08:00","id":1031871,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1031862#post1031862\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wish this Women's March luck, despite my skeptism that Trump will care, I don't want to see him work toward stripping away abortion rights.</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly, I don't think it's entirely about Trump. Politicians in general pay attention and if there is enough media coverage to help get their message out there... Part of the point of doing a March is to get people talking about it. Raising awareness and such.<br /> <br /> Hopefully, this helps them, whether Trump notices it or not.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/851-paradim","name":"Paradim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar851_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"695"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-14T18:16:34-08:00","id":1031904,"title":"","content":"The politicians who control the Senate and the House are Republicans, very few of whom will care either, they have no levage. Honestly I think sweeping the rot from the Democratic Party and building bridges with unhappy Trump voters should be the priority.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-14T18:43:14-08:00","id":1031910,"title":"","content":"I'm considering attending the march in Houston. Some of the people I follow on Facebook are bitching about the ban on anti-trump signs. While Trump is far from my favorite politician, I prefer these sort of gatherings be FOR something. Instead of against something.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-14T20:31:14-08:00","id":1031956,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wonderandawe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1031910#post1031910\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Some of the people I follow on Facebook are bitching about the ban on anti-trump signs.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm seeing a lot of that too. I don't like Trump, never have, never will, but Trump isn't the real issue. All of these problems existed before he made up his mind about running for office, let alone somehow winning the election. By focusing on him, the actual issues the protest is about will be ignored and instead of a women's rights march, it'll be viewed as an anti-Trump march. And one of many. Keep on message, stick to the message, and maybe you can change some minds.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writing up Clanbook: Aabbt","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/700-vysha","name":"Vysha","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar700_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"602"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-14T21:34:31-08:00","id":1031977,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wonderandawe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1031910#post1031910\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm considering attending the march in Houston. Some of the people I follow on Facebook are bitching about the ban on anti-trump signs. While Trump is far from my favorite politician, I prefer these sort of gatherings be FOR something. Instead of against something.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, they need to generally try and keep the march positive, or at least as positive as they can. Allowing anti-Trump signs risks having the march turn into (or at least be viewed as) an anti-Trump march rather than a pro-women's march which wouldn't be very good. In the end it's better to get people on your side with a positive message, than drive people away with a negative one. Plus, it also doesn't hurt to remember that just over 40% of women in America who voted, voted for Trump.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-15T08:33:20-08:00","id":1032142,"title":"","content":"I don't understand, who banned anti Trump signs?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-15T08:38:57-08:00","id":1032143,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032142#post1032142\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't understand, who banned anti Trump signs?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The organizers of the Houston Women's March asked that the protester not bring Anti-Trump signs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-15T09:55:26-08:00","id":1032181,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1031737#post1031737\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Again why has this thread become about me?</div> </div> </div> </div>Because you came into a thread about a feminist march and said &quot;what about men?&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":11}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-15T13:43:15-08:00","id":1032306,"title":"","content":"Hopefully, this is a start in getting protests that go beyond papier-m\u00e2ch\u00e9 parodies of politicians and mindless sloganeering, and get to having a coherent agenda and message without a contingent of flakes that (of course) every news/cameraperson will focus on to the exclusion of the main issues. What worked in the 1960s and 1970s will not work as well today, and using some of the smarter tactics of the Tea Party and flash mobs and such (without the deliberate Nazi-ass tactics) will go a long way to getting people to think about the issues before shutting down. <br /> <br /> I'm really grateful to see they've firmed up the message and don't seem interested in watering it down. That'll go a long way to being helpful. I don't care if they talk about &quot;the menz&quot; or not; I care that, as far as human rights go, if it's acceptable to treat women as full human beings, men will benefit by default. The reverse has not, historically, held true. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2176-spectaculartentacular","name":"SpectacularTentacular","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2176_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"179"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-15T14:47:05-08:00","id":1032332,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SpectacularTentacular</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032306#post1032306\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hopefully, this is a start in getting protests that go beyond papier-m\u00e2ch\u00e9 parodies of politicians and mindless sloganeering, and get to having a coherent agenda and message without a contingent of flakes that (of course) every news/cameraperson will focus on to the exclusion of the main issues. What worked in the 1960s and 1970s will not work as well today, and using some of the smarter tactics of the Tea Party and flash mobs and such (without the deliberate Nazi-ass tactics) will go a long way to getting people to think about the issues before shutting down. <br /> <br /> I'm really grateful to see they've firmed up the message and don't seem interested in watering it down. That'll go a long way to being helpful. I don't care if they talk about &amp;quot;the menz&amp;quot; or not; I care that, as far as human rights go, if it's acceptable to treat women as full human beings, men will benefit by default. The reverse has not, historically, held true. </div> </div> </div> </div> No men won't benifit by default, but this isn't the thread for this discussion according to the mods, perhaps we can take it to the MRA thread, so instead I'll say that I am not sure who they are trying to influence, perhaps it's the general public, but in practice I think only the converted. But I thought this was supposed to be an anti Trump rally, so if its not, then I think no one will pay attention, I mean they not protesting a specific bill, politician, etc.., I mean it's mostly feel good platitudes so why would anyone, even the general public care about this I don't. Have fun. I don't have anything else to add, mostly because if this isn't an anti Trump rally, I have no interest in it, it's just shouting into the wind and will be forgotten my most people in a couple of days when it gets drowned out by some stupid ass Trump tweet or Trump scandal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-15T15:38:49-08:00","id":1032350,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SpectacularTentacular</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032306#post1032306\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hopefully, this is a start in getting protests that go beyond papier-m\u00e2ch\u00e9 parodies of politicians and mindless sloganeering, and get to having a coherent agenda and message without a contingent of flakes that (of course) every news/cameraperson will focus on to the exclusion of the main issues. What worked in the 1960s and 1970s will not work as well today, and using some of the smarter tactics of the Tea Party and flash mobs and such (without the deliberate Nazi-ass tactics) will go a long way to getting people to think about the issues before shutting down.</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly, I think that part of the problem with so many of the protest movement's we've seen today, is that they <i>don't</i> give enough respect to the methods of the protest movements in the 60's and 70's and to a large degree reject those kind of methods as unworkable in the modern world. I feel that the &quot;modern&quot; leaderless-style of movements that we've seen in the Tea Party, Occupy Wall Street, and BLM have been rather ineffective.<br /> <br /> Of those movements, the only one to be considered a resounding success is the Tea Party, largely because they ultimately chose leaders to represent themselves, came up with a coherent platform and decided to focus heavily on politics. In fact I would argue that the election of Donald Trump is, in some respects, the capstone of the Tea Party.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Occupy Wall Street largely fizzled out and while Bernie Sanders had a bit of success in tapping into the residual embers, it wasn't enough to get him anywhere.<br /> <br /> And BLM has been a very mixed bag, in large part because its lack of leadership prevents the movement from being able to disavow specific people, and simply ensures that the media will listen to whichever person is the loudest (and that person is very often not a good representative for the movement to the public at large). For many people, the murders of police officers by a couple of nuts and the riots in Baltimore are what they think of when they think of BLM.<br /> <br /> I think what we've seen is that any kind of social movement, if you want to enact long-term change, needs to ultimately end up coalescing around a group who can lead it and speak for it. Otherwise you just have a mob of individuals running around doing their own thing. There's a lot of movement and action to be sure, but it's not unified action and a lot of it ends up being fairly self-destructive and wasteful. That's is what happened to Occupy Wall Street and what is happening to BLM.<br /> <br /> So hopefully future protest movements look back to the 60's and 70's and realize that even in a world dominated by social media, there's still some great ideas about successful protest movements that should be taken out of that time period.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-15T15:46:28-08:00","id":1032352,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032332#post1032332\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> But I thought this was supposed to be an anti Trump rally, so if its not, then I think no one will pay attention, I mean they not protesting a specific bill, politician, etc.., I mean it's mostly feel good platitudes so why would anyone, even the general public care about this I don't. Have fun.</div> </div> </div> </div>It was never an anti-Trump movement. It was a.. let's call it a pro-liberal march - you can see <a href=\"https://www.womensmarch.com/principles/\" target=\"_blank\">their platform here</a> which is about civil rights, workers rights, womens rights, immigrants rights, people with disabilities rights, LGBT rights, etc. When you stop to look at it, I imagine most people in the generic public probably care about women's rights (1/2 of the generic public is women, right?) And they probably are about civil and workers rights (most people in the general public work, and everyone benefits from stronger civil rights).<br /> <br /> I mean, let me put it this way - why don't you care about stronger civil rights or stronger workers rights?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-15T15:51:10-08:00","id":1032354,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032332#post1032332\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But I thought this was supposed to be an anti Trump rally</div> </div> </div> </div>It was very specific about what it is. It's a feminism march. I cannot for the life of me get where you thought that meant it's an anti-Trump march.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032332#post1032332\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">why would anyone, even the general public care about this I don't.</div> </div> </div> </div>Some people in the general public actually care about the issues affecting women. And a march of the size and scope being discussed is pretty huge. It will attract attention. That attention will focus on what the groups are protesting. That's how protests work. If most people felt the way you seem to think they feel, the civil rights movement would have died an early death. But of course it didn't.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writing up Clanbook: Aabbt","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/700-vysha","name":"Vysha","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar700_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"602"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-15T16:16:24-08:00","id":1032365,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032350#post1032350\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Honestly, I think that part of the problem with so many of the protest movement's we've seen today, is that they <i>don't</i> give enough respect to the methods of the protest movements in the 60's and 70's and to a large degree reject those kind of methods as unworkable in the modern world. I feel that the &quot;modern&quot; leaderless-style of movements that we've seen in the Tea Party, Occupy Wall Street, and BLM have been rather ineffective.<br /> <br /> Of those movements, the only one to be considered a resounding success is the Tea Party, largely because they ultimately chose leaders to represent themselves, came up with a coherent platform and decided to focus heavily on politics. In fact I would argue that the election of Donald Trump is, in some respects, the capstone of the Tea Party.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Occupy Wall Street largely fizzled out and while Bernie Sanders had a bit of success in tapping into the residual embers, it wasn't enough to get him anywhere.<br /> <br /> And BLM has been a very mixed bag, in large part because its lack of leadership prevents the movement from being able to disavow specific people, and simply ensures that the media will listen to whichever person is the loudest (and that person is very often not a good representative for the movement to the public at large). For many people, the murders of police officers by a couple of nuts and the riots in Baltimore are what they think of when they think of BLM.<br /> <br /> I think what we've seen is that any kind of social movement, if you want to enact long-term change, needs to ultimately end up coalescing around a group who can lead it and speak for it. Otherwise you just have a mob of individuals running around doing their own thing. There's a lot of movement and action to be sure, but it's not unified action and a lot of it ends up being fairly self-destructive and wasteful. That's is what happened to Occupy Wall Street and what is happening to BLM.<br /> <br /> So hopefully future protest movements look back to the 60's and 70's and realize that even in a world dominated by social media, there's still some great ideas about successful protest movements that should be taken out of that time period.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I strongly disagree. T<span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">he old-school protests have become a cliche, all too easy to dismiss. </span></span></span>With few exceptions, the leaders of such movements have been co-opted or discredited.<br /> <br /> No unified leadership means no leaders to target. Black Lives Matter and other such movements don't have the media on their side, so they seem muted and ineffective, but they're still out there and quite capable. It remains to be seen if being leaderless diminishes their long-term effectiveness. They're not <i>seen</i> communicating and organizing with their membership, true, but that means that the Powers That Be have to work harder to keep track of them. It's a trade-off, but one that might make a positive difference. <br /> <br /> What could stand to happen is that the protests get even more <b>demonstrative</b>. The Powers That Be are already discrediting them and decrying their &quot;violence&quot;, so what do the protesters gain by being polite? Nothing. Keep it inclusive, but keep it on point and not sanitized for mass media. Make it heard. The opposition has made itself shameless and therefore somewhat immunized to the outrage of &quot;outsiders.&quot; We all need to get louder, and remind them just who has the most people. I'm pretty much convinced that, for the first time in a long time, we're going to have some very ugly protests before things get back to sensible again. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2176-spectaculartentacular","name":"SpectacularTentacular","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2176_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"179"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-15T16:35:43-08:00","id":1032373,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032332#post1032332\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> No men won't benifit by default, but this isn't the thread for this discussion according to the mods, perhaps we can take it to the MRA thread, so instead I'll say that I am not sure who they are trying to influence, perhaps it's the general public, but in practice I think only the converted. But I thought this was supposed to be an anti Trump rally, so if its not, then I think no one will pay attention, I mean they not protesting a specific bill, politician, etc.., I mean it's mostly feel good platitudes so why would anyone, even the general public care about this I don't. Have fun. I don't have anything else to add, mostly because if this isn't an anti Trump rally, I have no interest in it, it's just shouting into the wind and will be forgotten my most people in a couple of days when it gets drowned out by some stupid ass Trump tweet or Trump scandal.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Please never assume I'll happily step into an MRA thread. <br /> <br /> The <i>last</i> thing that these protesters need to do is draw any more attention to Cheeto Mussolini. <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">The media will cock it up to get controversy and ratings, and the GOP will try to discredit them; they know this already, so why even bother giving the Clown-in-Chief any more credit than he deserves?</span></span></span> They ignore him and present their goals in a brave and undiluted fashion, and they'll start persuading the directionless. The protesters need to be <i>for</i> something and <i>strongly</i> for it, and then people will get interested. <br /> <br /> Some may not, because it doesn't involve men, but who gives a shit? If the feminists get enough <i>women</i> back on board, they'll be much better off than they were, and the men who'll come along are probably already involved. Granted, the more intersectional the movements get, the better off they'll be for the future, but now is the time to focus like a laser on the important issues.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2176-spectaculartentacular","name":"SpectacularTentacular","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2176_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"179"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-15T16:51:57-08:00","id":1032384,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SpectacularTentacular</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032365#post1032365\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I strongly disagree. T<span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">he old-school protests have become a cliche, all too easy to dismiss. </span></span></span>With few exceptions, the leaders of such movements have been co-opted or discredited.<br /> <br /> No unified leadership means no leaders to target. Black Lives Matter and other such movements don't have the media on their side, so they seem muted and ineffective, but they're still out there and quite capable. It remains to be seen if being leaderless diminishes their long-term effectiveness. They're not <i>seen</i> communicating and organizing with their membership, true, but that means that the Powers That Be have to work harder to keep track of them. It's a trade-off, but one that might make a positive difference.<br /> <br /> What could stand to happen is that the protests get even more <b>demonstrative</b>. The Powers That Be are already discrediting them and decrying their &quot;violence&quot;, so what do the protesters gain by being polite? Nothing. Keep it inclusive, but keep it on point and not sanitized for mass media. Make it heard. The opposition has made itself shameless and therefore somewhat immunized to the outrage of &quot;outsiders.&quot; We all need to get louder, and remind them just who has the most people. I'm pretty much convinced that, for the first time in a long time, we're going to have some very ugly protests before things get back to sensible again.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Only time will tell, but I have a feeling that BLM, like Occupy, is going to burn out and end up in history's dustbin, where it will go down as largely as another failed social movement that managed to get a few high profile cases reviewed or overturned but which didn't have any deep-seated or long lasting effect on the US criminal justice system.<br /> <br /> Because the thing is if you want to effect change in America you need to get the majority of the population on your side, you need to get the political apparatus on your side and you need to get the laws on your side.<br /> <br /> During the 60's and 70's, the leaders of protest movements were able to get political leaders on their side. They would sit down with these people, have discussions, point out very clearly what they wanted and how it would help them. The leaders helped bring the politicians around to their side. With the Tea Party, members ended up running for office to get elected and that's how the Tea Party had such an effect on politics (though, to be fair, the Tea Party was specifically a political movement rather than a social movement).<br /> <br /> With Occupy and (so far) BLM, that hasn't happened. They made a lot of noise, but they haven't really been able to get politicians to do more than just smile and nod, in no small part because there was no coherent, specific message to present to politicians, and there were no leaders to engage those politicians to get their message across.<br /> <br /> Further both Occupy and BLM have done an incredibly poor job of getting your average American behind them. This was the key to the success for the Civil Rights movement - the leaders of the Civil Rights movement managed to convince white America that justice for all was in their interest too. BLM has not succeeded nearly as well and for the most part is viewed as black movement rather than as an American movement.<br /> <br /> I may have mentioned in another thread, I live in a predominantly Hispanic part of the country. Black Lives Matter has had pretty much zero impact here, even though many of the problems that African-Americans face at the hands of the police, the judicial system and society are the exact same problems Hispanics face. The thing is though, most Hispanics here (and most white people, and Asians, and Native-Americans, etc) see Black Lives Matter as a black movement, and since they're not black, it doesn't have anything to do with them.<br /> <br /> Just being loud and trying to be heard isn't enough. Anyone can be loud. But if you don't have a strong message and you can't convince the majority of America to get behind you, and you can't convince the media to get behind you, and you can't convince individual politicians to get behind you because you don't have a coherent agenda, then you won't be seeing any particularly positive changes.<br /> <br /> And worse, every time there's an outbreak of violence and no leaders to disavow it, or the media can't find any leaders to interview so they just set up meetings with whoever says, &quot;Yeah I'm in charge&quot; and that person turns out to be a nut, that's not going to help the movement at all.<br /> <br /> I think there are good reasons to have a leaderless movement, but at a certain point a movement needs leaders and it needs a specific, coherent message. Without those then a movement is just a big, loud, leaderless mob and nobody particularly likes those.<br /> <br /> I'm just saying this as someone who thinks BLM should absolutely succeed, that our criminal justice system absolutely needs to be fixed, and who thinks that seeing BLM ineffectively burn out will be one of the most tragic and unfortunate things I will witness during my lifetime.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-15T17:00:36-08:00","id":1032389,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> During the 60's and 70's, the leaders of protest movements were able to get political leaders on their side. They would sit down with these people, have discussions, point out very clearly what they wanted and how it would help them. The leaders helped bring the politicians around to their side. </div> </div> </div>The problem is, the novelty of protests has worn off. We already had the 1960's-1970s. Those in charge (soon to be in charge, to be accurate) were hit the hardest by those original protests, and have spent years with their allies in media and advertising to immunize the public from protests like what we saw. We need a new style of protest, because the old ways aren't going to work against a savvier, less willing to compromise opposition. This new bunch has ironed out most of the kinks in its control of the narrative, and will continue to control the narrative, and won't let the protesters get ahead the way they did before.<br /> <br /> And I frankly doubt that the American public were ever going to get behind Occupy and BLM. Not until things got bad enough that even they couldn't ignore it. That point is getting closer every day.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2176,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2176-spectaculartentacular","user":"SpectacularTentacular","date":". 01-15-2017, 09:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2176-spectaculartentacular","name":"SpectacularTentacular","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2176_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"179"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-15T18:17:44-08:00","id":1032418,"title":"","content":"Again though, if you want to effect massive social change on powerful institutions, you either need to convince the majority of society to get on your side, or you have to convince enough politicians to get on your side, such that they are willing to enact legislation to help you.<br /> <br /> The methods of the Civil Rights era managed to accomplish both of this, and in large part is was because its leaders managed to speak with the average American and to politicians. I didn't see anything comparable from Occupy and so far haven't seen anything similar from BLM. There's a bunch of very loud noise but very little actual communication with the greater public.<br /> <br /> Honestly, I think the lack of leadership has ended up crippling the movement - many average Americans have no idea what BLM wants. They think of BLM as a terrorist organization. They hear anti-police rhetoric, hear about people shooting police officers, see the riots in Baltimore and say, &quot;Oh, that's what BLM is about.&quot; And without any leaders who can step up and discuss the movement, it's not viewed as a legitimate one by most people.<br /> <br /> The US media leans to the left a fair bit, and in the early stages of BLM there were many attempts to reach out to the movement, but the lack of leaders made it hard for news organizations to do more than report on what was <i>happening</i> rather than what BLM <i>wanted to have happen</i>, and what was happening was often fairly negative (especially with the outbreak of violence) and there were no leaders or figures who could go to the media, stand up for BLM and condemn that violence.<br /> <br /> At this point in many parts of the country you're far more likely to hear All Lives Matter or even Blue Lives Matter than to hear someone say Black Lives Matter.<br /> <br /> I'm not saying that you have to mimic the Civil Rights protests of the 60's and 70's to succeed with protest movements, but without leaders or other designated representatives to serve as intermediaries between a movement and the rest of the population, that movement will have a lot of difficulty in being embraced and accepted by the general public - and if the general public doesn't get on board with that movement, then I don't know how that movement will succeed at the massive social changes it seeks to have occur.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":741,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","user":"AnubisXy","date":". 01-15-2017, 10:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum