, I live in a predominantly Hispanic part of the country. Black Lives Matter has had pretty much zero impact here, even though many of the problems that African-Americans face at the hands of the police, the judicial system and society are the exact same problems Hispanics face. The thing is though, most Hispanics here (and most white people, and Asians, and Native-Americans, etc) see Black Lives Matter as a black movement, and since they're not black, it doesn't have anything to do with them.<br /> <br /> Just being loud and trying to be heard isn't enough. Anyone can be loud. But if you don't have a strong message and you can't convince the majority of America to get behind you, and you can't convince the media to get behind you, and you can't convince individual politicians to get behind you because you don't have a coherent agenda, then you won't be seeing any particularly positive changes.<br /> <br /> And worse, every time there's an outbreak of violence and no leaders to disavow it, or the media can't find any leaders to interview so they just set up meetings with whoever says, &amp;quot;Yeah I'm in charge&amp;quot; and that person turns out to be a nut, that's not going to help the movement at all.<br /> <br /> I think there are good reasons to have a leaderless movement, but at a certain point a movement needs leaders and it needs a specific, coherent message. Without those then a movement is just a big, loud, leaderless mob and nobody particularly likes those.<br /> <br /> I'm just saying this as someone who thinks BLM should absolutely succeed, that our criminal justice system absolutely needs to be fixed, and who thinks that seeing BLM ineffectively burn out will be one of the most tragic and unfortunate things I will witness during my lifetime.</div> </div> </div> </div> Bernie Sanders had original set up a meeting with BLM during the Primaries, they had his fucking ear, what do that do right before they we're to meet with him, they sabotaged his speech, take the Mike from him, humiliate him, make him look weak, helped give the Hillary camp the material to make Bernie Sanders look like a racist after he naturally got pissed off at them, a man involved in the civil rights movement. <br /> <br /> And when they took the Mike from Hillary and fucked up her speech... Oh wait they never did that to Hillary, they helped her own, Hillary picked up most minority votes in the Primaries, which helped, her beat Sanders, which lead to her losing to President Stop and Frisk, I mean talk about scoring on your own goal, every African American and Hispanic Democract and Independent should hate BLM.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-15T22:46:09-08:00","id":1032493,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032471#post1032471\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Bernie Sanders had original set up a meeting with BLM during the Primaries, they had his fucking ear, what do that do right before they we're to meet with him, they sabotaged his speech, take the Mike from him, humiliate him, make him look weak, helped give the Hillary camp the material to make Bernie Sanders look like a racist after he naturally got pissed off at them, a man involved in the civil rights movement.</div> </div> </div> </div>But did they? Did every single person claiming to be a member of BLM meet Bernie? Did they all take his mic? Seriously, you need to stop treating a <i><b>leaderless movement</b></i> as a <i><b>single individual</b></i>. It doesn't work like that. Stop judging an <i><b>entire movement</b></i> based on a handful of individuals.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writing up Clanbook: Aabbt","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/700-vysha","name":"Vysha","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar700_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"602"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-15T23:05:31-08:00","id":1032501,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vysha</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032493#post1032493\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But did they? Did every single person claiming to be a member of BLM meet Bernie? Did they all take his mic? Seriously, you need to stop treating a <i><b>leaderless movement</b></i> as a <i><b>single individual</b></i>. It doesn't work like that. Stop judging an <i><b>entire movement</b></i> based on a handful of individuals.</div> </div> </div> </div>Those people were members of Black Lives Matter and as such, <i>by the way the movement is designed</i> their actions speak for and represent Black Lives Matter movement just as much as any other members' actions do. This is also one of the big problems a leaderless social movement that doesn't have anyone who officially speaks for it.<br /> <br /> To put it another way, Black Lives Matter is proud to call itself a <b>leaderful</b> movement for which anyone can speak for it, or claim their actions represent it, and that's how Black Lives Matter is designed to work<i>.</i> Any members actions and words are just as valid, and represent Black Lives Mater, just as much as anyone else's do.<br /> <br /> So you end up with incidents like what happened with Bernie Sanders, where a bunch of jerks decided to bully him, and ended up hurting the movement's standing in the eyes of a lot of people. And saying, that, &quot;it was Black Lives Matter that did this&quot; is entirely correct because, as a &quot;leaderful&quot; movement, any member has the right to claim to speak for the movement.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":741,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","user":"AnubisXy","date":". 01-16-2017, 02:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-15T23:32:59-08:00","id":1032510,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032471#post1032471\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Bernie Sanders had original set up a meeting with BLM during the Primaries, they had his fucking ear, what do that do right before they we're to meet with him, they sabotaged his speech, take the Mike from him, humiliate him, make him look weak, helped give the Hillary camp the material to make Bernie Sanders look like a racist after he naturally got pissed off at them, a man involved in the civil rights movement.<br /> <br /> And when they took the Mike from Hillary and fucked up her speech... Oh wait they never did that to Hillary, they helped her own, Hillary picked up most minority votes in the Primaries, which helped, her beat Sanders, which lead to her losing to President Stop and Frisk, I mean talk about scoring on your own goal, every African American and Hispanic Democract and Independent should hate BLM.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're grossly oversimplifying a nuanced dynamic. Not that I'm surprised.<br /> <br /> Sanders had set up a meeting with leaders of a particular BLM group, it wasn't with any sort of national leadership because as has been mentioned previously BLM doesn't really have that kind of structure. The BLM group that hijacked his rally in Seattle wasn't the same group he had a meeting with. Even then, after the media blip it created Sanders made greater efforts to include race issues in his platform and I would argue that between him and Clinton he was the candidate closer to BLM as political ally. At the same time, BLM groups made similar overtures to the Clinton campaign but were stymied by having to negotiate the machinery of the congressional black caucus and other &quot;traditional&quot; black leadership groups. There were attempts by BLM groups to create similar visible disruptions of Clinton appearances, but they generally failed to accomplish much beyond a couple news blips because Clinton's campaign was much tighter organized.<br /> <br /> Clinton garnered more black votes in the primaries because Clinton had substantially greater cache in the realm of black politics. Before she was a New York Senator she was First Lady of Arkansas. She moves in the world and converses in the language of black community organization far more comfortably than Sanders, who despite his civil rights history and voting record came from an overwhelmingly white state and tended to do best in similarly white states. Clinton also represented a brand far more than Sanders did. In addition to her association with the Obama administration and working relationship with organs of black political leadership Clinton held a strong nostalgic appeal for older black voters (who voted in far greater numbers than their younger, more pro-Sanders cohort). While the legacy of her husband's presidency is mixed in terms of its long term impact on black Americans, black households saw their income grow from the 90's economic boom at a rate almost twice that of other demographics. The Clintons also had a reputation for appointing African Americans to political positions going back through the 80's.<br /> <br /> You're also assuming that Sanders was the obviously more viable candidate in the general election, which wasn't apparent then and isn't even apparent now.<br /> <br /> Really though, keep popping off about what African Americans should hate. I mean its not like they have any idea what the landscape of their own political culture is or anything.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-15T23:57:44-08:00","id":1032521,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032501#post1032501\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Those people were members of Black Lives Matter and as such, <i>by the way the movement is designed</i> their actions speak for and represent Black Lives Matter movement just as much as any other members' actions do. This is also one of the big problems a leaderless social movement that doesn't have anyone who officially speaks for it.<br /> <br /> To put it another way, Black Lives Matter is proud to call itself a <b>leaderful</b> movement for which anyone can speak for it, or claim their actions represent it, and that's how Black Lives Matter is designed to work<i>.</i> Any members actions and words are just as valid, and represent Black Lives Mater, just as much as anyone else's do.<br /> <br /> So you end up with incidents like what happened with Bernie Sanders, where a bunch of jerks decided to bully him, and ended up hurting the movement's standing in the eyes of a lot of people. And saying, that, &quot;it was Black Lives Matter that did this&quot; is entirely correct because, as a &quot;leaderful&quot; movement, any member has the right to claim to speak for the movement.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is such a closed- and narrow-minded way of looking at it, and you already know it. The actions of an individual never speak for the actions of a whole. Otherwise, you wouldn't have members of BLM speaking out against the more extremist members. All your really trying to say is &quot;leaderless movements are prone to badness&quot; and we already know that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writing up Clanbook: Aabbt","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/700-vysha","name":"Vysha","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar700_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"602"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T00:26:39-08:00","id":1032525,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vysha</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032521#post1032521\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That is such a closed- and narrow-minded way of looking at it, and you already know it. The actions of an individual never speak for the actions of a whole. Otherwise, you wouldn't have members of BLM speaking out against the more extremist members. All your really trying to say is &quot;leaderless movements are prone to badness&quot; and we already know that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Again though, that's the entire point of BLM's leaderless/leaderful movement, that there is no leadership - or rather, that every member is a leader and representative of the movement.<br /> <br /> The woman who shut down Bernie Sanders speech (Marissa Johnson) was one of the co-founders of Seattle's Black Lives Matter chapter, and helped set it up almost a year before the incident with Bernie Sanders. There were a lot of members of Black Lives Matter who were very supportive of how she and others handled Bernie Sanders. She literally embodies the idea of a &quot;leaderful&quot; movement of Black Lives Matter in which local individuals have the power to speak for the movement.<br /> <br /> If anybody can be said to speak for Black Lives Matter (and again, by design anyone <i>can</i> be said to speak for it), it's someone like Marissa Johnson.<br /> <br /> So to say she represents Black Lives Matter is accurate, and at the same time to say she <i>doesn't</i> represent Black Lives Matter is also accurate. She's as much of a representative and leader as anyone in BLM (and as a co-founder of a chapter probably a bit more of a leader than quite a few BLM members to be fair). That is, again, one of the big flaws in the movement.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T00:42:20-08:00","id":1032531,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Caitiff Primogen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032510#post1032510\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're also assuming that Sanders was the obviously more viable candidate in the general election, which wasn't apparent then and isn't even apparent now.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I think the qualities that helped Clinton lock in the Democratic nomination (her past actions and record) are also what caused her to lose in the general election. I mean while Bernie Sanders might not have cared about her emails, after the FBI reopened its investigation, it turned out that a whole lot of other Americans cared.<br /> <br /> At the very least a Sanders/Trump election would have played out in a completely different way from the Trump/Clinton one. And while we don't know if Bernie could have beaten Trump, we do know that Clinton didn't beat him, and many of the biggest obstacles she ran into during the election were ones she (and her husband) had dragged along with them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T00:47:12-08:00","id":1032533,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032525#post1032525\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Again though, that's the entire point of BLM's leaderless/leaderful movement (And more words)</div> </div> </div> </div>That is all great and wonderful and not something I was even discussing or disputing. If you want to discuss <i><b>my</b></i> opinions and views, address <i><b>them </b></i>and not pretend to address them while trying to reiterate a point you'd already been making to someone else. My point was saying the entirety of BLM had acted against B.S. and so forth is not true. A branch did, certainly. And some people not affiliated with that branch certainly backed up those actions. But it was neither all nor even a majority. So, yeah.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writing up Clanbook: Aabbt","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/700-vysha","name":"Vysha","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar700_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"602"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T06:56:53-08:00","id":1032628,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032471#post1032471\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Bernie Sanders had original set up a meeting with BLM during the Primaries, they had his fucking ear, what do that do right before they we're to meet with him, they sabotaged his speech, take the Mike from him, humiliate him, make him look weak, helped give the Hillary camp the material to make Bernie Sanders look like a racist after he naturally got pissed off at them, a man involved in the civil rights movement.<br /> <br /> And when they took the Mike from Hillary and fucked up her speech... Oh wait they never did that to Hillary, they helped her own, Hillary picked up most minority votes in the Primaries, which helped, her beat Sanders, which lead to her losing to President Stop and Frisk, I mean talk about scoring on your own goal, every African American and Hispanic Democract and Independent should hate BLM.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think even the more aggressive members of BLM would have done such antics with Hillary Clinton, because as a previous First Lady, she's got a Secret Service detail guarding her at these rallies and they won't tolerate anything like aggressive people coming up onto the stage with her on it. The Secret Service is an intimidating factor because they have guns and if you do something that they think is a threat to their ward, they <i>will</i> bring you down. Which is a factor that Bernie Sanders didn't really have at his rallies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/851-paradim","name":"Paradim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar851_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"695"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T07:53:30-08:00","id":1032659,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vysha</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032533#post1032533\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That is all great and wonderful and not something I was even discussing or disputing. If you want to discuss <i><b>my</b></i> opinions and views, address <i><b>them </b></i>and not pretend to address them while trying to reiterate a point you'd already been making to someone else. My point was saying the entirety of BLM had acted against B.S. and so forth is not true. A branch did, certainly. And some people not affiliated with that branch certainly backed up those actions. But it was neither all nor even a majority. So, yeah.</div> </div> </div> </div> Show me the BLM activists who condemned it and the Settle Branch! <br /> <br /> And you keep saying there are no leaders in BLM, my first thought was you we're being niave, there are, always leaders even if no such official positions exist, there are always people who have the most influence. Then I realized it wasn't niave, it was purposeful that way BLM can never be held to account for any of it's actions ever. BLM does something bad, just say it's a leaderless movement, and the bad thing doesn't represent it, even if the bad thing gets massive support from all over BLM. <br /> <br /> None of your arguments change the fact that if BLM didn't exist President Stop and Frisk wouldn't be in office, because everything dominoes from that action, it's as far as I can tell that the only actual &quot;achievement&quot; of BLM has been to help President Stop and Frisk to win, by helping him avoid running against his real Foe, Bernie Sanders.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T08:25:56-08:00","id":1032681,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> None of your arguments change the fact that if BLM didn't exist President Stop and Frisk wouldn't be in office, because everything dominoes from that action, it's as far as I can tell that the only actual &amp;quot;achievement&amp;quot; of BLM has been to help President Stop and Frisk to win, by helping him avoid running against his real Foe, Bernie Sanders.</div> </div> </div> </div>You didn't actually read my post did you?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T09:25:27-08:00","id":1032707,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032659#post1032659\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">None of your arguments change the fact that if BLM didn't exist President Stop and Frisk wouldn't be in office, because everything dominoes from that action, it's as far as I can tell that the only actual &quot;achievement&quot; of BLM has been to help President Stop and Frisk to win, by helping him avoid running against his real Foe, Bernie Sanders.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, he still probably would have won. Bernie did not have enough mass-media exposure at any point in his campaign. Trump had a megalithic media campaign, enhanced by liberals like us turning him into a meme right from day one.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032659#post1032659\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And you keep saying there are no leaders in BLM, my first thought was you we're being niave, there are, always leaders even if no such official positions exist, there are always people who have the most influence. Then I realized it wasn't niave, it was purposeful that way BLM can never be held to account for any of it's actions ever. BLM does something bad, just say it's a leaderless movement, and the bad thing doesn't represent it, even if the bad thing gets massive support from all over BLM.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm sorry, but that is some pretty heavy tinfoil hat logic. I have nothing more to say to you, because we're clearly not working within the same framework of reason, logic and reality. Good day, sir.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writing up Clanbook: Aabbt","edited":{"userid":700,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/700-vysha","user":"Vysha","date":". 01-16-2017, 12:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/700-vysha","name":"Vysha","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar700_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"602"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T09:43:00-08:00","id":1032728,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Caitiff Primogen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032681#post1032681\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You didn't actually read my post did you?</div> </div> </div> </div>What were you expecting?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T10:16:28-08:00","id":1032749,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vysha</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032533#post1032533\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That is all great and wonderful and not something I was even discussing or disputing. If you want to discuss <i><b>my</b></i> opinions and views, address <i><b>them </b></i>and not pretend to address them while trying to reiterate a point you'd already been making to someone else. My point was saying the entirety of BLM had acted against B.S. and so forth is not true. A branch did, certainly. And some people not affiliated with that branch certainly backed up those actions. But it was neither all nor even a majority. So, yeah.</div> </div> </div> </div>BLM is literally designed so that anyone can speak for it. To say she spoke for BLM is absolutely not wrong. BLM as a movement allows any person to speak for it, and when they do, that is &quot;working as intended.&quot; That is how a &quot;leaderfull&quot; movement (which BLM specifically calls itself out as) functions - that every person is a leader and has the right to speak for the movement. <br /> <br /> Yes it sucks when some people take advantage of that and when they decide to use their power to speak for the movement, they decide to say and do some scummy stuff. And I'm sorry you don't like that particular aspect of BLM, but when something like that happens, again it's working as intended.<br /> <br /> If the BLM movement doesn't like the fact that people such as Marissa Johnson can speak for it and represent it, then the movement should take steps so that people like Marissa Johnson aren't entitled to speak for it. But so far, the movement has not done that. Which, again, that goes back to my earlier point on why BLM, as a &quot;leaderfull&quot; organization, has has been doing so poorly in crossing into mainstream America.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T14:36:52-08:00","id":1032921,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032749#post1032749\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes it sucks when some people take advantage of that and when they decide to use their power to speak for the movement, they decide to say and do some scummy stuff. And I'm sorry you don't like that particular aspect of BLM, but when something like that happens, again it's working as intended.</div> </div> </div> </div>And yet, when people apply the same logic to the &quot;leaderfull&quot; and formally leaderless American conservative movement... it's the horrible liberals tarring and feathering good conservatives for the actions of the radical extremes.<br /> <br /> So I guess maybe it sucks when people say that this past election season proves that conservatives in this country have willfully embraced sexism, and instead of complaining about how unfair that generalization is, you should just recognize that it's the conservative movement working as intended.<br /> <br /> Wait... aren't we supposed to be talking about a march about women's rights?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T15:15:07-08:00","id":1032943,"title":"","content":"Trying to not make this statement too pointed at any particular groups or individuals: People have a tendency to look at the negative actions of subsets of movements they do not belong to as representative of the movement as a whole, while resenting when people do the same to organizations they do belong to. I think we would all do well not to call kettles black.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T15:33:33-08:00","id":1032949,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1032921#post1032921\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And yet, when people apply the same logic to the &quot;leaderfull&quot; and formally leaderless American conservative movement... it's the horrible liberals tarring and feathering good conservatives for the actions of the radical extremes.<br /> <br /> So I guess maybe it sucks when people say that this past election season proves that conservatives in this country have willfully embraced sexism, and instead of complaining about how unfair that generalization is, you should just recognize that it's the conservative movement working as intended.<br /> <br /> Wait... aren't we supposed to be talking about a march about women's rights?</div> </div> </div> </div>Back when the Tea Party was a leaderless movement and people were bringing their racist posters and saying terrible stuff, that was the Tea Party - then they nominated leaders and started getting things done. The recent election of Donald Trump can, perhaps, be viewed as the ultimate success on the part of the Tea Party to influence the American political landscape. The Tea Party is no longer a &quot;leaderfull&quot; organization in the same way it was when it was created, and the politicians that it elects are who now speak for the movement.<br /> <br /> Again though, as I pointed out earlier, the Tea Party was nevert a social movement, it was a <i>political</i> movement - the goal was always to change the American political landscape (in fact really, the landscape of one political party), and the leaders they elected tended to have very clear ideas on how to do that. Meanwhile BLM has specifically chosen to avoid engaging in the political process as they feel that not only is it a lost cause, but that by getting behind politicians that would be tantamount to selecting leaders and they would no longer be a leaderfull organization.<br /> <br /> Which is, you know, a problem, because watching BLM implode due to its anarchic system is really a damn shame. Especially if many of the things BLM is fighting for and trying to change get dismissed by mainstream society because of the way the negative actions of a few BLM's members can speak for the entire movement.<br /> <br /> But yeah, we're having some tread drift (though BLM does claim to fight for women's rights as well, so the two are related). Still, we can drop it if you want.<br /> <br /> I was just discussing it because watching BLM fizzle out will probably be one of the most tragic and unfortunate things I have to see happen during my lifetime.<br /> <br /> In contrast, I would not have been at all sad had the Tea Party imploded because, you know, I think their political values are fairly abhorrent in most ways. However seeing police and other authority figures mistreat members of minorities is terrible and if that continues to happen because BLM's self-defeating policies and fear of &quot;leadership&quot; prevented mainstream America and politicians from taking them seriously? That will truly be unfortunate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":741,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","user":"AnubisXy","date":". 01-16-2017, 06:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T17:01:08-08:00","id":1033006,"title":"","content":"I don't recall using the words &quot;Tea Party&quot; in the post you responded to. And of course you seem to be very intent on ignoring anything I said in it, esp. the last part.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T17:23:14-08:00","id":1033025,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1033006#post1033006\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't recall using the words &quot;Tea Party&quot; in the post you responded to. And of course you seem to be very intent on ignoring anything I said in it, esp. the last part.</div> </div> </div> </div>I admit, since you didn't actually mention any names, I assumed you meant the Tea Party since that's probably the best example of a formerly leaderfull conservative movement in America that was &quot;tarred and feathered&quot; by liberals due to the actions of radical extremists (though again, the Tea Party deserved every bit of that because as a similar organization those radical extremists were also representative of that movement).<br /> <br /> But if that wasn't Tea Party you were talking about, then out of curiosity, which was the former leaderfull conservative movement that you were referring to?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T17:31:15-08:00","id":1033035,"title":"","content":"I already said exactly what I meant. I don't see how I can specify it further, you can just stop applying assumptions and take it at its face.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T17:36:12-08:00","id":1033038,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1033035#post1033035\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I already said exactly what I meant. I don't see how I can specify it further, you can just stop applying assumptions and take it at its face.</div> </div> </div> </div>There have been several formerly leaderless conservative movements in the US, so I was simply a bit confused as to which one you were referring to. I apologize if you took umbrage with my asking for a bit of clarity. Regardless, have a nice rest of your day anyway.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T18:42:00-08:00","id":1033084,"title":"","content":"I'll clarify the point where you seem to continue to think I said &quot;former&quot; where I said, &quot;formal.&quot;<br /> <br /> There rest isn't you asking me for actual clarity on what I said. It's your own embracing of an incoherent stance biting itself in the ass. Or, in other words, I feel that the only way I can satisfy your desire for &quot;clarity&quot; is to phrase things that diminishes my own point so you can avoid confronting the failings in your own.<br /> <br /> Also, again, none of this has to do with marching for women's rights, and you've yet to address that; which requires no clarification on my part.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-16T19:39:37-08:00","id":1033101,"title":"","content":"Gotcha.<br /> <br /> Though I admit, I'm still not clear <i>which</i> formally leaderless American conservative movement you're talking about. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Most specific human-led movements and organizations have a name (Occupy Wall Street, the Tea Party, Black Lives Matter, Move-On.org, the Alt-Right) so without you naming the movement, it's kind of hard to know which one you're talking about.<br /> <br /> I Googled &quot;American Conservative Movement&quot; and it just brought up links to conservative ideology rather than any kind of formal movement or organization. Is that the name of a specific human-led movement organization? Or were you just discussing a general political ideology? Because that's where I'm confused on.<br /> <br /> If the &quot;American conservative movement&quot; is a leaderfull organization that empowers anyone who claims membership to speak on its behalf, then anyone who says jerk stuff while speaking for that movement is... speaking for the movement when they say jerk stuff, just like with BLM, the early Tea Party and other leaderfull organizations/movements.<br /> <br /> If what you were discussing is an ideology however, an ideology in and of itself is not an organization and therefore can't really choose leaders or empower specific people to speak for it on its behalf. The Spirit of Liberalism can't come down from the heavens and say, &quot;I empower Bernie Sanders and Elizabeth Warren to speak for American liberalism.&quot;<br /> <br /> But unlike an ideology, an organization or human-driven movement <i>can</i> choose specific people to represent it and when they choose people of poor character as representatives they're naturally going to suffer the consequences (and when you let <i>anyone</i> speak for your movement, you're naturally going to end up with a lot of poor characters representing your movement).<br /> <br /> There are probably lots of organizations and specific movements within the American conservative ideology (the Republican Party, the Tea Party, the Libertarian Party, various whack-job patriot groups, certain veteran's organizations, etc). <i>Those</i> specific organizations or movements can empower people to speak on their behalf (whether that's a specific person or just anyone). But the ideology of American conservatism itself can't really do that.<br /> <br /> So honestly, I'm just kind of confused as to whether you were talking about a specific organization or an ideology. Hopefully that clears up where I'm coming from.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":741,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","user":"AnubisXy","date":". 01-17-2017, 04:35 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-17T02:09:35-08:00","id":1033242,"title":"","content":"&quot;Mainstream&quot; America needing some sort of pleasing introduction to the idea of &quot;Stop systematic racism within the judicial/law enforcement system.&quot; tends to say more to me about the nature of mainstream America than it does about the nature of Black Lives Matter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-17T05:08:17-08:00","id":1033270,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1033035#post1033035\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I already said exactly what I meant. I don't see how I can specify it further, you can just stop applying assumptions and take it at its face.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you didn't mean the Tea Party, then I've got no clue either what &quot;leaderful&quot; and formally leaderless conservative movement you meant. It's not clear, very vague and not helpful to not clarify if someone is clearly not understanding what you meant. <br /> <br /> Specifically to ask, what conservative movement are you talking about there?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/851-paradim","name":"Paradim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar851_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"695"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-17T15:12:44-08:00","id":1033674,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TyrannicalRabbit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1033242#post1033242\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;Mainstream&quot; America needing some sort of pleasing introduction to the idea of &quot;Stop systematic racism within the judicial/law enforcement system.&quot; tends to say more to me about the nature of mainstream America than it does about the nature of Black Lives Matter.</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly I think it comes down to a certain amount of naivety and willful ignorance on the part of the American public. In general I've found that Americans tend to have a somewhat more simplistic outlook on many things than people in other countries. Americans are often quick to put people in simple categories - they are especially fond of &quot;good guys in white hats and bad guys in black hats.<br /> <br /> Many Americans grow up with this innate belief that the police are &quot;good guys.&quot; So when they hear about an incident involving a police officer shooting an unarmed black man or what have you, those Americans (having this ingrained belief that the police are good guys) tend to naturally start with the idea that the officer was in the right. And if it does come out that the police officer did something wrong, most Americans see it as a one-off incident, a case of a &quot;bad apple.&quot; They'll say something like, &quot;Throw that dirty cop in prison and call it a day.&quot;<br /> <br /> The problem, of course, is that many Americans don't want to recognize that the problems run a lot deeper than just a couple of bad apples, and that many of these problems are systematic and even institutionalized in many areas, and therefore the nature of our police system (and indeed judicial system as well) are in dire need of reform.<br /> <br /> Basically I think what it comes down to is that may Americans are kind of naive and simply don't want to believe that these kind of incidents (while not directly related) do represent an overall systemic problem with our police system.<br /> <br /> They're not out there willfully saying, &quot;Screw black people,&quot; but rather, &quot;Most police officers are good people who aren't abusing anyone and aren't engaging in racist behavior. If any police officer does do something like that, it's because that person was a bad person and he should be punished. But don't punish the good guys because of the actions of a few bad ones.&quot;<br /> <br /> This is, I think, the core of the issue that mainstream America needs to realize and that hopefully BLM (and other similar movements) will do a better of of convincing people - that the police are just human, and saying that there is widespread racism and other problems endemic to our system does not mean that <i>all</i> of the police (or even most of the police) are bad people.<br /> <br /> Sometimes there are no good guy or bad guys - sometimes there are just problems that need to be fixed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":741,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","user":"AnubisXy","date":". 01-17-2017, 07:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-17T15:23:06-08:00","id":1033681,"title":"","content":"For that matter, a lot of Americans seem to have a lot of trouble thinking in terms of systems. Our culture is <i>so</i> individualistic, any talk of systemic issues comes across to many people as overgeneralizing, and is met with &quot;but not all [whatever]!&quot;<br /> <br /> Yes. We know not all [whatever], now can we please stop reiterating the obvious and talk about the system-level issues that are leading many (but not all) [whatever] to [bad thing]?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-17T17:03:39-08:00","id":1033735,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1033674#post1033674\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Honestly I think it comes down to a certain amount of naivety and willful ignorance on the part of the American public. In general I've found that Americans tend to have a somewhat more simplistic outlook on many things than people in other countries. Americans are often quick to put people in simple categories - they are especially fond of &quot;good guys in white hats and bad guys in black hats.<br /> <br /> Many Americans grow up with this innate belief that the police are &quot;good guys.&quot; So when they hear about an incident involving a police officer shooting an unarmed black man or what have you, those Americans (having this ingrained belief that the police are good guys) tend to naturally start with the idea that the officer was in the right. And if it does come out that the police officer did something wrong, most Americans see it as a one-off incident, a case of a &quot;bad apple.&quot; They'll say something like, &quot;Throw that dirty cop in prison and call it a day.&quot;<br /> <br /> The problem, of course, is that many Americans don't want to recognize that the problems run a lot deeper than just a couple of bad apples, and that many of these problems are systematic and even institutionalized in many areas, and therefore the nature of our police system (and indeed judicial system as well) are in dire need of reform.<br /> <br /> Basically I think what it comes down to is that may Americans are kind of naive and simply don't want to believe that these kind of incidents (while not directly related) do represent an overall systemic problem with our police system.<br /> <br /> They're not out there willfully saying, &quot;Screw black people,&quot; but rather, &quot;Most police officers are good people who aren't abusing anyone and aren't engaging in racist behavior. If any police officer does do something like that, it's because that person was a bad person and he should be punished. But don't punish the good guys because of the actions of a few bad ones.&quot;<br /> <br /> This is, I think, the core of the issue that mainstream America needs to realize and that hopefully BLM (and other similar movements) will do a better of of convincing people - that the police are just human, and saying that there is widespread racism and other problems endemic to our system does not mean that <i>all</i> of the police (or even most of the police) are bad people.<br /> <br /> Sometimes there are no good guy or bad guys - sometimes there are just problems that need to be fixed.</div> </div> </div> </div>What probably disturbs me the most is what so often appears to be the abject lack of will to even look below the surface of what has caused these systematic issues and their connection to poverty and class warfare, as well as the reoccurring narrative I see of some that liberals are trying to disempower (insert sexual or ethnic minority) here through trying to point out systematic abuses even going so far as the canard of how Liberals, Democrats,etc are the &quot;real racists!&quot; for doing so. It's what worries the most about the state of affairs in America. While it's hardly the only place in the world suffering this issue there seems to me to be such an ingrained normalization of certain forms of corruption in areas of politics and the judicial/law enforcement systems that it's reached that point where only painful, unsettling upheaval will result in dismantling it. Not what I wish, but what I fear will be.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2997,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/","user":"Guest","date":". 01-19-2017, 01:46 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T07:47:54-08:00","id":1034011,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1033681#post1033681\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For that matter, a lot of Americans seem to have a lot of trouble thinking in terms of systems. Our culture is <i>so</i> individualistic, any talk of systemic issues comes across to many people as overgeneralizing, and is met with &quot;but not all [whatever]!&quot;<br /> <br /> Yes. We know not all [whatever], now can we please stop reiterating the obvious and talk about the system-level issues that are leading many (but not all) [whatever] to [bad thing]?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think part of it has to do with the fact that systems and institutions are more difficult to wrap your head around. At least that's a stumbling block that I've encountered in debates. Even many people who's stated goal is dismantling systemic, institutionalized oppression can still tend to think in very individual and personalized terms. Its much easier to imagine a singular living architect of inequality than it is to imagine a nebulous aggregate of decisions made by mostly well-meaning and rational actors but inflected with enough invisible bias that they create an edifice of unequal policy outcomes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T19:14:14-08:00","id":1034405,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1033681#post1033681\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For that matter, a lot of Americans seem to have a lot of trouble thinking in terms of systems. Our culture is <i>so</i> individualistic, any talk of systemic issues comes across to many people as overgeneralizing, and is met with &quot;but not all [whatever]!&quot;<br /> <br /> Yes. We know not all [whatever], now can we please stop reiterating the obvious and talk about the system-level issues that are leading many (but not all) [whatever] to [bad thing]?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, but Charlaquin, that all sounds like a whole lot of work to fix something that doesn't really involve me and it sounds way to difficult too bother understanding in the first place. Plus, hearing about all this stuff really bums me out and I sort of wish everyone would just stop talking about it. How about instead I just like a BLM post on Facebook (which I didn't read) and wish everyone who isn't me or part of my immediate social circle good luck? <br /> <br /> Probably not very helpful, but hey, at least I don't feel my cis-hetero-white-male-upper class guilt anymore. So you know, there's that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1675-weirdboyz","name":"Weirdboyz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1675_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"212"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T21:21:14-08:00","id":1034448,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Weirdboyz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1034405#post1034405\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yeah, but Charlaquin, that all sounds like a whole lot of work to fix something that doesn't really involve me and it sounds way to difficult too bother understanding in the first place. Plus, hearing about all this stuff really bums me out and I sort of wish everyone would just stop talking about it. How about instead I just like a BLM post on Facebook (which I didn't read) and wish everyone who isn't me or part of my immediate social circle good luck? <br /> <br /> Probably not very helpful, but hey, at least I don't feel my cis-hetero-white-male-upper class guilt anymore. So you know, there's that.</div> </div> </div> </div>That about sums it up, yeah.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-19T03:39:35-08:00","id":1034510,"title":"","content":"Does BLM have any concrete proposals as to solve the issue of killing unarmed black men by cops? <br /> <br /> Because I have a few. Body Cams.<br /> <br /> More funding for developing none lethal tools for capturing suspects. <br /> <br /> End the Drug War<br /> <br /> Legalize sex work. <br /> <br /> End other victimless crimes. <br /> <br /> Look to jurisdictions that have improved in this area and have those areas with the worst records try and see if any of those methods could be adopted by them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-19T12:12:01-08:00","id":1034768,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1034510#post1034510\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Body Cams.</div> </div> </div> </div>Body cams are good, the problem is that much like relying on people flipping on cameras to record abusive cops, you only get part of the story if you don't run them continually. There's still power/memory issues around them in addition to the MUCH larger legal ramifications... is it legal to have the police record everything every officer does and use it compile automatic suspect lists, of say people with identified political leanings or a habit of going to protests?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1034510#post1034510\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">More funding for developing none lethal tools for capturing suspects.</div> </div> </div> </div> Eeeeh, I'm very much of two minds about this. A lot of money has been tossed down the hole of &quot;non lethal weapons&quot; over the decades for relatively little to show for it, and much of what has resulted are things of questionable moral use such as the microwave guns. I would far rather increase the training and proper equipment use that is available before trying to develop new toys.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1034510#post1034510\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">End the Drug War</div> </div> </div> </div> While I agree it would dramatically improve violent crime and drug gangs (less demand in street drugs, less profit in street drugs, less street drug gangs) I see two big problems... one the sheer amount of money and infrastructure already invested into it and thus the organizations that gain from it. Second is that it eliminates the big easy profit that lures people into gangs and drug-sale related violence, but it doesn't actually do anything to help the fact that in poor areas people go into drug sales/production because it's seen as the best alternative available.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1034510#post1034510\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Legalize sex work.</div> </div> </div> </div> Again, something I'd agree with in an ideal situation, but with the current US cultural influences I don't see this as being generally accepted by the public, for either the basis that violence IS common in the profession and nobody wants to endorse violence against women and the whole religious influence factors.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1034510#post1034510\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">End other victimless crimes.</div> </div> </div> </div> Personally I'm quite fond of the idea of a system of sliding fines myself. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1034510#post1034510\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Look to jurisdictions that have improved in this area and have those areas with the worst records try and see if any of those methods could be adopted by them.</div> </div> </div> </div>It gets tricky when you do this particular sort of comparison because what something says it is on paper is often not what is actually enforced. Three different areas each launching a tough on crime initiative might handle it by increased traffic tickets, searching people in the streets, or trying to solve any major crime within 8 hours of it being reported.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-19T14:41:10-08:00","id":1034871,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1031175-the-women-s-march-on-washington?p=1034768#post1034768\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Body cams are good, the problem is that much like relying on people flipping on cameras to record abusive cops, you only get part of the story if you don't run them continually. There's still power/memory issues around them in addition to the MUCH larger legal ramifications... is it legal to have the police record everything every officer does and use it compile automatic suspect lists, of say people with identified political leanings or a habit of going to protests?<br /> <br /> Eeeeh, I'm very much of two minds about this. A lot of money has been tossed down the hole of &amp;quot;non lethal weapons&amp;quot; over the decades for relatively little to show for it, and much of what has resulted are things of questionable moral use such as the microwave guns. I would far rather increase the training and proper equipment use that is available before trying to develop new toys.<br /> <br /> While I agree it would dramatically improve violent crime and drug gangs (less demand in street drugs, less proenfosup , get drugs, less street drug gangs) I see two big problems... one the sheer amount of money and infrastructure already invested into it and thus the organizations that gain from it. Second is that it eliminates the big easy profit that lures people into gangs and drug-sale related violence, but it doesn't actually do anything to help the fact that in poor areas people go into drug sales/production because it's seen as the best alternative available.<br /> <br /> Again, something I'd agree with in an ideal situation, but with the current US cultural influences I don't see this as being generally accepted by the public, for either the basis that violence IS common in the profession and nobody wants to endorse violence against women and the whole religious influence factors.<br /> <br /> Personally I'm quite fond of the idea of a system of sliding fines myself. <br /> <br /> <br /> It gets tricky when you do this particular sort of comparison because what something says it is on paper is often not what is actually enforced. Three different areas each launching a tough on crime initiative might handle it by increased traffic tickets, searching people in the streets, or trying to solve any major crime within 8 hours of it being reported.</div> </div> </div> </div> You do realize that there are male prostitutes right? <br /> <br /> And the current legal frame does NOTHING to protect prostitutes from violence, in fact the exact opposite it endangers them. Johns and prostitites who see bad things don't come forward out of fear, hired bodyguards are punished for providing security, some good clients are driven away by law enforcement pushing sex workers to take riskier jobs, etc...<br /> <br /> You do you who it does help? The massive profits of the rescue industry. The massive profits of the prison slavery industry. <br /> <br /> It doesn't help sex workers, it's does help the owners of the facilities, it doesn't help the people who work for the sex workers, it doesn't help they're children, it doesn't the clients, it helps the parasites who pretend they are mortally greater then the people actually involved, and the fucked up jerks who exploit they're legislated vulneriblity of the people involved to hurt them.<br /> <br /> If prostitution abolitionists really wanted to reduce the amount of prostitution, especially survival prostitution, they'd push for BMI which would end most of the survival prostitution and reduce other forms as well, instead of banning it and tormenting the people involved. <br /> <br /> Why don't they? Because their is no profit in it for themselves and in fact it would cost them a great deal of money as there would be much less survival prostitution. <br /> <br /> End the drug war and spend that money on finding a cure for drug addiction and on rehabilitation. <br /> <br /> As for researching non lethal weapons, we haven't found the perfect cure for cancer either,but humanity keeps on trying, and I have enough faith in human imagination that eventually we will succeed on both fronts someday if given the time and resources.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-20T07:44:45-08:00","id":1035219,"title":"","content":"I'm stuck here at work, on my birthday. Pant Suit Republic - Houston is showing a lot of pictures of full flights of protesters. Makes my heart swell to see all those woman fly out to March on Washington. I'm seeing a lot of my Facebook friends from various parts of the country signal they are attending protests.<br /> <br /> Probably won't make it to the Houston March. I may have to work. We will see.<br /> <br /> Edit: If I do make to the march, I think I found my protest poster: <a href=\"http://www.tor.com/2017/01/20/general-leia-organa-resistance-poster/\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.tor.com/2017/01/20/genera...stance-poster/</a>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":3950,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","user":"wonderandawe","date":". 01-20-2017, 11:07 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-20T08:38:15-08:00","id":1035243,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-